Skip the BoringSSL fiat-p256 ADX assembly path on Windows MinGW.

The fiat_p256_adx_mul/sqr implementations in
third_party/fiat/asm/fiat_p256_adx_{mul,sqr}.S are guarded with
`(defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__ELF__))`, so they never define their
symbols on Windows (PE/COFF). The callers in p256_64.h, however, were
gated only on `__GNUC__ && __x86_64__`, which is true under MinGW —
producing undefined references to `fiat_p256_adx_mul` / `fiat_p256_adx_sqr`
when linking transport_security_state_generator, root_store_tool, and
QtWebEngineCore.

Tighten the caller gates in p256_64.h to match the .S files'
`(__APPLE__ || __ELF__)` guard so MinGW falls through to the C
implementation, matching what the MSVC build does.

diff --git a/src/3rdparty/chromium/third_party/boringssl/src/third_party/fiat/p256_64.h b/src/3rdparty/chromium/third_party/boringssl/src/third_party/fiat/p256_64.h
--- a/src/3rdparty/chromium/third_party/boringssl/src/third_party/fiat/p256_64.h
+++ b/src/3rdparty/chromium/third_party/boringssl/src/third_party/fiat/p256_64.h
@@ -1,7 +1,8 @@
 #include <openssl/base.h>
 #include "../../crypto/internal.h"

-#if !defined(OPENSSL_NO_ASM) && defined(__GNUC__) && defined(__x86_64__)
+#if !defined(OPENSSL_NO_ASM) && defined(__GNUC__) && defined(__x86_64__) && \
+    (defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__ELF__))
 extern "C" {
 void fiat_p256_adx_mul(uint64_t*, const uint64_t*, const uint64_t*);
 void fiat_p256_adx_sqr(uint64_t*, const uint64_t*);
